{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>חַיּוֹת</b> — to exclude טְרֵפוֹת (birds with mortal defects). <b>טְהוֹרוֹת</b> — to exclude unclean birds. Because the nega'im (afflictions) come upon evil speech, which is an act of פָּטִיט (chatter), therefore for his purification he required birds that constantly chatter with chirping sound — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 14:4). And since they said \"טְהוֹרוֹת to exclude unclean birds,\" it may be inferred that the birds are not a specific known species, but rather it is a comprehensive term for all birds. If so, what is this פִּטְפּוּט (chirping) that they found for them, for many birds do not open their mouth and chirp. And furthermore, the midrash \"חַיּוֹת to exclude טְרֵפוֹת\" will be subject to dispute, for according to the one who says a טְרֵפָה is [nevertheless] a חַיָּה, this is not so. And in Torat Kohanim (parashah 1, 12): \"חַיּוֹת\" — not שְׁחוּטוֹת (slaughtered); \"טְהוֹרוֹת\" — not טְמֵאוֹת; \"טְהוֹרוֹת\" — not טְרֵפוֹת.\n\nAnd the masters of the plain sense say that every bird is called צִפּוֹר, from what is said: \"צִפּוֹר שָׁמַיִם וּדְגֵי הַיָּם\" (Psalm 8:9); \"כֹּל צִפּוֹר כָּל כָּנָף\" (Genesis 7:14); \"בֶּן אָדָם אֱמֹר לְצִפּוֹר כָּל כָּנָף\" (Ezekiel 39:17); and likewise \"וְאֶת הַצִּפֹּר לֹא בָתָר\" (Genesis 15:10), regarding תּוֹרִים and בְּנֵי יוֹנָה.\n\nAnd the correct view in my eyes is that the term צִפּוֹר is a general term for the small birds that rise early in the morning to chirp and sing, from the Aramaic root צַפְרָא (morning/dawn); and likewise \"יָשׁוּב וְיִצְפֹּר\" (Judges 7:3) means he will rise early in the morning. Scripture said \"צִפּוֹר שָׁמַיִם\" about them because for the most part they fly aloft in the sky. And \"כֹּל צִפּוֹר כָּל כָּנָף\" (Genesis 7:14) refers to two categories — all the small ones and all the large ones. \"כִּי יִקָּרֵא קַן צִפּוֹר לְפָנֶיךָ\" (Deuteronomy 22:6) — these are the small ones that are numerous, for He has compassion even on their smallest. And likewise \"אֲשֶׁר שָׁם צִפֳּרִים יְקַנֵּנוּ\" (Psalm 104:17), for they are the ones that dwell in the branches of the cedars of Lebanon. \"אֱמֹר לְצִפּוֹר כָּל כָּנָף\" (Ezekiel 39:17) — that even the small ones will gather upon them, for the predators will come of themselves. And likewise \"הַתְשַׂחֶק בּוֹ כַּצִּפּוֹר וְתִקְשְׁרֶנּוּ לְנַעֲרוֹתֶיךָ\" (Job 40:29), for it is the way of children to play with a small bird.\n\nAnd so is the language of the Sages: \"Anyone who has in his hand a staff or a צִפּוֹר\" (Avodah Zarah 40a); \"one who weaves from the hair of a nazirite an amount the size of a sit in a garment, in צִפַרְתָּא\" (Temurah 34a); and they spoke of צִפֹּרֶת כְּרָמִים (Shabbat 90a); and they said: the flesh of צִפֳּרִים restores the sick person to his illness (Berakhot 57b). And Scripture says (Deuteronomy 14:11): \"כָּל צִפּוֹר טְהֹרָה תֹּאכֵלוּ\" — concerning all those many species, and the bird-offering of the מְצֹרָע is derived from \"כָּל\" (all). And it says (ibid. v. 12): \"וְזֶה אֲשֶׁר לֹא תֹאכְלוּ מֵהֶם\" — as if to say, \"and these are those whose flesh you shall not eat,\" and therefore they expounded \"טְהוֹרוֹת — not טְמֵאוֹת.\" In any case, all of them are possessors of פִּטְפּוּט. And so likewise: \"גַּם צִפּוֹר מָצְאָה בַיִת וּדְרוֹר קֵן לָהּ\" (Psalm 84:4) — thus it appears that it is not a comprehensive term for all birds, and likewise \"אֲשֶׁר שָׁם צִפֳּרִים יְקַנֵּנוּ חֲסִידָה בְּרוֹשִׁים בֵּיתָהּ\" (Psalm 104:17).\n\nAnd what appears from the words of our Sages is that all clean birds are called \"צִפּוֹר,\" but the mitzvah of the מְצֹרָע is specifically with צִפֳּרֵי דְּרוֹר, as it is taught in Torat Kohanim (chapter 5, 14): \"וְשִׁלַּח אֶת הַצִּפֹּר הַחַיָּה אֶל מִחוּץ לָעִיר אֶל פְּנֵי הַשָּׂדֶה\" (Leviticus 14:53) — Rabbi Yosi the Galilean says: a bird that lives outside every city, and which one is that? The דְּרוֹר. And from this midrash they mentioned the פִּטְפּוּט. And it is possible that this is merely for the mitzvah (ideal), but after the fact (בְּדִיעֲבַד) all are valid for it, and therefore Torat Kohanim was required to exclude טְמֵאוֹת. And so we learned in the Mishnah, tractate Nega'im (14:1): \"and he brings two דְּרוֹר birds.\" And they further taught there (Mishnah 5): \"The two birds — their mitzvah is that they be alike in appearance, in size, and in value, and that they be purchased together; even if they are not alike, they are valid. If he slaughtered one of them and it was found not to be a דְּרוֹר, he must take a pair for the second one.\" And the reason for this is that even though after the fact (בְּדִיעֲבַד) all are valid, when they are of two species they are invalid.\n\nAnd in the chapter אֵלּוּ טְרֵפוֹת (Hullin 62b) they said: a bird that pecks (הַמְסָרֵט) is valid for purifying the מְצֹרָע, and this is the white swallow, regarding which Rabbi Eliezer and the Sages disagreed. From here it follows that the birds of the מְצֹרָע are not one species alone, and that one does not purify with every clean bird — rather, the mitzvah is with any דְּרוֹר bird, meaning one that dwells in a house as in a field. Therefore they said regarding the swallow: since it is clean according to the Sages, it is valid for purification, for it is included in the category of דְּרוֹר. In any case, all clean birds are valid after the fact (בְּדִיעֲבַד), for they are all included in \"two clean birds.\" And they taught in the Sifrei (Re'eh 103): Rabbi Yoshiya said: Everywhere Scripture says \"צִפּוֹר,\" the verse speaks of a clean one. And Rabbi Yitzhak said: a clean bird is called \"עוֹף\" and is called \"צִפּוֹר,\" but an unclean one is called only \"עוֹף.\" And so they mentioned in the Gemara in the chapter Shilluach ha-Ken (Hullin 139a), and there they raised (ibid. 140a) that \"חַיּוֹת\" — whose limbs are alive — excludes those missing a limb. And טְרֵפוֹת are likewise invalid. And they expounded \"טְהוֹרוֹת\" — to exclude those forbidden, such as the birds of an idolatrous city (עִיר הַנִּדַּחַת) and a bird that killed a person, and substitutes for an animal dedicated to idolatry. And their midrash there derives from the language itself — that צִפּוֹר means only a clean bird — and it implies from this that every clean bird is included in צִפּוֹר.\n\nAnd I saw further in the Jerusalem Talmud, tractate Nazir (1:1), where they said: \"Does a defiled nazirite bring צִפֳּרִים? He brings תּוֹרִים and בְּנֵי יוֹנָה!\" — There are tanna'im who teach: every clean bird is called צִפֳּרִים; there are tanna'im who teach: all birds, whether unclean or clean, are called צִפֳּרִים. And so a dispute remains in our hands from it. In any case, it is possible that the term applies to the small ones alone.\n\nAnd in the Gemara it thus appears, from what they said in tractate Sotah (16a): \"Bring water in which the blood of a צִפּוֹר is discernible\" — and how much? A revi'it. And they asked: a large one, whose blood displaces the water, is known; a small one, which is displaced by the water — what? And they explained: all the measurements of the Sages are thus — the Sages measured with a צִפּוֹר דְּרוֹר; you have none larger that displaces the water, and none smaller that is displaced by the water. But if every clean bird were valid, there would be among them one whose blood displaces many lugim. But perhaps they said this regarding the צִפּוֹר דְּרוֹר, which is the ideal מִצְוָה (לְכַתְּחִלָּה), from the midrash of Rabbi Yosi the Galilean. And they already mentioned in the Gemara (Shevuot 29a): \"But perhaps he saw a large bird and called it by the name 'camel.'\"\n\nAnd what should emerge from all this is that we say that any bird that is not a דְּרוֹר is invalid even after the fact (בְּדִיעֲבַד), from this midrash — for they did not teach in our Mishnah (Nega'im 14:1): \"their mitzvah is that they be דְּרוֹר; even though they are not דְּרוֹר, they are valid\" — as they taught regarding being alike (ibid. 14:5). And all דְּרוֹר birds are possessors of פִּטְפּוּט.\n\nAnd what they said in Torat Kohanim, \"לֹא טְמֵאוֹת\" — is because even among the unclean there are species that are דְּרוֹר, such as the swallow according to Rabbi Eliezer. Or its meaning may be to exclude those טְמֵאוֹת for you, which are the forbidden ones and the טְרֵפוֹת, as they raised in the Gemara in the chapter Shilluach ha-Ken (Hullin 140a). And this is what is proper in my eyes.\n\nAnd in the Aggadah of Rabbah (Leviticus Rabbah 16:7), Rabbi Yehuda bar Rabbi Simon said: \"These birds cry aloud\" — this refers to one who speaks evil speech. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: let a voice come and atone for a voice. And Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: \"Birds — צִפֳּרֵי דְּרוֹר\" — which ate from His bread and drank from His waters. And is this not an a fortiori argument (קַל וָחֹמֶר), etc." "<b>וְעֵץ אֶרֶז וּשְׁנִי תוֹלַעַת וְאֵזֹב</b> — Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 14:4): these are the greatest and the smallest of the species of plants, and the proof is from the words of Solomon's wisdom (1 Kings 5:13). And behold, the מְצֹרָע and the law of the afflicted house and the impurity of the dead are related, and behold, they resemble the Passover of Egypt. And the reason for \"אֶל פְּנֵי הַשָּׂדֶה\" (Leviticus 14:7) is a place where there is no settlement, so that the צָרַעַת will not adhere.\n\nAnd in Torat Kohanim (parashah 2, 5) they said: \"אֶל פְּנֵי הַשָּׂדֶה\" — that he should not stand in Jaffa and send it to the sea, nor stand in Gvat and send it to the wilderness. If so, the reason would be like the principle of the scapegoat (שָׂעִיר הַמִּשְׁתַּלֵּחַ) — there it is sent to Azazel in the wilderness, and here, to the open fields. And I will explain this further (Ramban on Leviticus 16:8), with the help of Heaven."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The Aramaic צַפְרָא literally means 'morning'; Ramban suggests the term צִפּוֹר derives from this root because small birds chirp at dawn.", 1 "The reference to 1 Kings 5:13 in Ibn Ezra's comment corresponds to 1 Kings 4:33 in some numbering systems; the verse describes Solomon's knowledge of trees from the cedar to the hyssop."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:25:01.495131661Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}